  "body": "Found by the review of PR #3689. Split from #3692.\n\n## What happens\n\nIn a mm program, the Haas post rounds each feed to a whole number of mm/min. For a G84 tapping cycle the feed is pitch x spindle speed. The control cuts the pitch F / S. A rounded F gives a wrong pitch.\n\nExamples:\n\n- M1.4 x 0.3 at S115: the exact feed is 34.5. The post writes `F35.`. The pitch error is 1.45 percent, about 0.04 mm of axial drift in 10 turns.\n- M2 x 0.4 at S333: the exact feed is 133.2. The post writes `F133.`. The pitch error is 0.15 percent.\n\nThe rounding is older than PR #3689. That PR did not change it.\n\n## Why it is wrong\n\nThe posted feed does not agree with the thread pitch. The program passes with no warning. The result can be a damaged thread or a broken tap.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe F word of a G84 (and G74) block gives the exact feed for the pitch and the speed. Options:\n\n1. Write the tap feed with three decimals in mm (four in inch).\n2. Refuse a pitch and speed pair whose feed the format cannot write exactly.\n\n## Done means\n\n- A test pins the F word of G84 for the two examples above, mm and inch.\n- The check covers the other post scripts that round a tap feed. Each one found gets its own issue or its fix here, with the list in a comment.\n- The decision file names the option taken and the public source for the feed format of the control.\n\nRefs #3681, #3692, #3690.\n",
